<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Tālava</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1224 CE (approx.) · capital Beverīna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Latgalian principality in north-eastern Latvia whose ruler Tālivaldis allied with the German crusaders against the Estonians.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tālava was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgalians">Latgalian</a> territory in what is now north-eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Latvia</a>, with its main stronghold at Beverīna. It appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-livonia">Henry of Livonia's</a> chronicle in the early 13th century, when it paid tribute to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a> and was ruled by the elder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talivaldis">Tālivaldis</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Tālava's rulers led a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgalians">Latgalian</a> society of farmers and warriors with hillforts. Facing raids from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonians">Estonians</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sakala">Sakala</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ugandi">Ugandi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talivaldis">Tālivaldis</a> and his sons Rameķis and Varibulis accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> baptism around 1208 and joined the Germans and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a> in campaigns northwards.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1215 raiders from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sakala">Sakala</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talivaldis">Tālivaldis</a> and roasted him to death. His sons kept a subordinate role, but in 1224 Tālava was divided between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Riga and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Sword Brothers</a>. It became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/terra-mariana">Terra Mariana</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgalians">Latgalians</a> merged into the Latvian people.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/talava.jpg" alt="Tālava" width="320" height="164"><figcaption>Image: Ainārs · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Talava.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1224 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Beverīna</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tālivaldis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Baltic paganism, later Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latgalian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/latvia">Latvia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tolowa, Talava</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1162&amp;focus=talava" aria-label="Tālava on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5226 231 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tālava lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5546 487L5664 460"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5664 460h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5546 487h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5546 487h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1162&amp;focus=talava">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

